  "reasoning_content": "We need answer user: \"Explain how a relational database executes a SQL query, from parsing and planning through indexing, joins, transactions, and returning rows. Write a detailed technical tutorial with concrete examples and tradeoffs, including common performance pitfalls. Use complete sentences and avoid repeating yourself.\"\n\nNeed produce detailed technical tutorial. Need final only. Need be thorough, complete sentences, avoid repetition. Need cover parsing, planning, indexing, joins, transactions, returning rows. Concrete examples and tradeoffs, common performance pitfalls.\n\nNeed structure. Could use headings. Need ensure complete sentences. Avoid repeating. Let's plan.\n\nTitle: How a relational database executes a SQL query.\n\nSections:\n1. High-level pipeline: client sends SQL, server parses, validates, optimizes, executes, returns.\n2. Parsing and validation: lexical analysis, syntax tree, semantic analysis, name resolution, type checking, privilege checks, prepared statements. Example: SELECT ... FROM orders o JOIN customers c ON o.customer_id=c.id WHERE c.country='US' AND o.status='shipped' ORDER BY o.created_at DESC LIMIT 100.\n3. Query planning/optimization: logical plan, physical plan, cost model, statistics, cardinality estimates, access paths, join order, join algorithms, index selection, predicate pushdown, projection pushdown, common subexpression, materialization. Explain optimizer chooses based on cost. Tradeoffs: cost-based vs rule-based, stale stats, misestimates, forced indexes, plan caching.\n4. Indexing: B-tree, hash, covering, composite, partial, expression, GIN/GiST maybe. How indexes help point lookups, range scans, joins, sorts. Example indexes. Tradeoffs: write amplification, storage, maintenance, selectivity, index-only scans, index scans vs seq scans. Pitfalls: low selectivity, leading wildcard, function on column, implicit type conversion, wrong column order, too many indexes, index fragmentation, missing covering columns.\n5. Execution: physical operators. Scan, filter, join, aggregate, sort, limit. Need explain join algorithms: nested loop, hash join, merge join. Tradeoffs and when used. Example join. Index nested loop vs hash join. Also aggregation: hash aggregate vs sort aggregate. Sort: external sort if memory. Limit: early termination.\n6. Transactions and concurrency: MVCC, locks, isolation levels, snapshot, write-ahead log, commit/rollback. How query sees data. Example",
